{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>To speak in Your name, etc. — and You have not saved, etc.</b> Meaning: besides the earlier arguments, he further complains before the King — how can this thing be? For ever since I spoke to this wicked man in Your name, not only did he do nothing, but he added further evil. And he is precise in saying <b>מֵאָז</b> — meaning, from the very hour itself that he mentioned the name of HaShem, blessed be He, to him, he became filled with wrath. For through this the matter can be recognized: what he did was from his hatred of HaShem and from his diminishing His worth in his eyes — he intended to disparage, heaven forbid. And there is none who lifts a hand against the King and touches His honor like this. And Moses intended through this to say: how could El Elyon not be provoked for the honor of His name, to save Israel from his hand, to stop him from doing evil to His people immediately and at once — even if Israel on their own merit were not worthy of a miracle, they would be worthy for the sake of His name, blessed be He, which the wicked man profaned in the arrogance of his heart. And this is his saying <b>וְהַצֵּל לֹא הִצַּלְתָּ</b>, in astonishment — how did You not save them for the sake of Your name which he despised? And he doubled the expression with two forms of הצלה, to allude that even without this reason they stand in need of salvation, and for this reason He sent him."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:55.787645220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}